#pragma once

#include "V34Scrambler.h"

#include <utility>

namespace voipmodem::dsp {

// ITU-T V.34 (02/98) clause 10.1.3.8, verbatim:
//
//   "Signal TRN is a sequence of symbols generated by applying binary
//   ones to the input of the scrambler described in clause 7. The
//   scrambled bits are mapped to a 4- or 16-point 2D constellation
//   depending on the signal J.
//
//   The 4-point TRN signal is generated by using two scrambled bits,
//   I1n and I2n, which are transmitted every 2D symbol interval, where
//   I1n is the first bit in time. The transmitted points are obtained
//   by rotating point 0 from the quarter-superconstellation of Figure 5
//   clockwise by In * 90 degrees, where In = 2 * I2n + I1n.
//
//   The 16-point TRN signal is generated by using four scrambled bits,
//   I1n, I2n, Q1n, and Q2n, which are transmitted every 2D symbol
//   interval and I1n is the first bit in time. The transmitted points
//   are obtained by using integer 2 * Q2n + Q1n to select a point from
//   the quarter-superconstellation of Figure 5 and then rotating that
//   point clockwise by In * 90 degrees, where In = 2 * I2n + I1n.
//
//   The scrambler is initialized to zero prior to transmission of the
//   TRN signal."
//
// TRN is the signal a real V.34 modem transmits during Phase 3 so the
// far end's adaptive equalizer/echo canceller can train against a
// known, scrambler-derived (pseudorandom-looking but fully
// deterministic) symbol sequence -- this is "the echo-cancelling
// training" a person listening to the line would actually hear.
//
// This class produces the exact sequence of (I, Q) constellation points
// (in Figure 5's integer grid units, same as V34Constellation.h) that a
// real modem would transmit. It does NOT yet produce audio: turning
// these symbols into a waveform needs a pulse-shaping QAM modulator
// driven at the negotiated symbol rate and carrier (clause 9.3/9.4),
// which does not exist yet -- see docs/ROADMAP.md.
//
// I1n contributes the LOW bit of In (weight 1) even though it is
// extracted from the scrambler FIRST in time and I2n (extracted
// second) contributes the HIGH bit (weight 2); that is what the
// spec's own formula In = 2*I2n + I1n says, and it matches this
// project's established LSB-first bit-packing convention elsewhere
// (see V34Info1.cpp's AppendBitsLsbFirst). Likewise for the 16-point
// case, Q1n/Q2n (extracted third/fourth) form the base-point index
// 2*Q2n + Q1n into kQuarterConstellation[0..3].
class V34TrnGenerator {
public:
    V34TrnGenerator(V34ScramblerMode mode, bool use16Point);

    // Re-initializes the scrambler to zero, per 10.1.3.8.
    void Reset();

    // Advances by one 2D symbol interval and returns the transmitted
    // constellation point for it.
    std::pair<int, int> NextPoint();

private:
    V34Scrambler scrambler_;
    bool use16Point_;
};

} // namespace voipmodem::dsp
